      "title": "Common Countable and Uncountable Nouns",
      "objective": "Distinguish singular and plural countable nouns from uncountable nouns.",
      "description": "Countable nouns can have a number; uncountable nouns describe a substance or mass.",
      "content": "Use a or an with one countable item, plural forms with numbers, and no plural -s for common uncountable substances.",
      "examples": [
        "I bought an apple.",
        "We need some rice."
      ],
      "commonErrors": [
        "Do not say two milks for the substance.",
        "Use a or an only with a singular countable noun."
      ],
      "guidedPractice": "Ask whether the noun can be counted as separate items before choosing its form.",
